Follow-up: -DTRACE_VARIANTS=4 adds omitted-queue and pinned-submitter controls.
The installed binary prints nr_hw_queues=2 when -q is omitted, on both Images.
All cases retain the original fio flags; only the last uses taskset -c 0.
                    original bpf / independent completion order breaks
                       GCC                  EvoCC
explicit one queue     0 / 0                0 / 0
explicit two queues   14 / 22               3 / 5
default (two queues)  10 / 14              12 / 19
two queues, pin fio    0 / 0                0 / 0
Every case has 131,072 sequential issue events, every sector completed exactly
once, zero trace drops/errors, fio exit zero, and normal nonce-bound shutdown.
Unpinned two-queue completions span CPUs 0/2; pinned completions only CPU 0.
This explains the failed strict global-order assertion for the actual default
and explicit multi-queue setup; it does not prove every possible kernel I/O path.
The differing bpf/tracefs counts additionally mean that the unsynchronized
global map is not an exact event-order counter. No kernel fix is inferred.

Unchanged originals replayed afterward under their original 45-second budget:
  generic_01: GCC FAIL, EvoCC FAIL
  generic_02: GCC PASS, EvoCC FAIL
All four boots shut down; no backing file changes. Together with the earlier
opposite intermittent generic_02 result, this is a scheduling-dependent upstream
order assumption, not evidence of an EvoCC-only defect. K04b classification is
closed; historical and new original FAILs remain FAIL, and K04/K13 remain OPEN.
No test is patched or silently pinned in the acceptance lane.

